id: knockback
name: Knockback
kind: signature
side: advantages
tpCost: 4
requirement: N/A
description: >
  Your technique pushes the opponent away.
text: >
  If you successfully Damage an Opponent with this Signature Technique, after the
  Wound Roll, you may make a Might Clash. If you win, move the target a number of
  Squares in a straight line away from you in any direction up to your Might.

---

# No script, for the same reason Charging Assault has none: what this does is move
# a character, and the language has no verb for that.
#
# What the system does is open the Clash and get out of the way. It is a Might
# Clash - Might on both sides, which is one of the four categories a Clash can be,
# and the only one of the four that is a single value rather than a family. Offered
# to the attacker after the Wound Roll and only when Damage was actually dealt,
# which is the condition the rule opens with, and offered rather than fired: "you
# may make a Might Clash" is a choice.
#
# Winning it is where the system stops. The direction is "any direction away from
# you", the distance is "up to your Might", and both are the attacker's to pick on
# the map - the Squares are right there to count.
#
# What the movement causes is Collision Damage, which is a Life Point reduction:
# it goes straight off Life, past the Soak Value and past Damage Reduction. The
# Clash card offers it as a button, to whoever won and only then - there is no
# movement without the win and no collision without the movement.
#
# How much it is depends on what they hit and how far they went, so it is asked
# rather than derived, and asked once per Clash. Two characters thrown by one
# Maneuver each have a Clash of their own and are each asked their own number:
# they did not hit the same wall.
#
# The Launching Profile doubles it, and says so itself rather than being named
# here - an Advantage does not know which Profile handed it out.
